# novadeck trim removal list — the seal's size counterpart.
#
# The explicit, reviewed declaration of what rootfs/trim-rootfs.sh deletes from the RELEASE root
# after the seal. Editing this file changes what ships, so it is committed and reviewed like
# rootfs/conf/seal.list and rootfs/manifest.lock.
#
# WHY THIS IS SEPARATE FROM rootfs/conf/seal.list: the seal removes the package MANAGER, and its
# argument is about what a sealed root must not be able to do to itself. This file removes BUILD
# AND DOCUMENTATION ARTEFACTS, and its argument is about size — every megabyte here is paid twice
# under A/B updates, once in the slot and once in every RAUC bundle that carries it. Different
# reasons, different review criteria, so different files.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT: it is not a way to un-install a package. Everything named here belongs to a
# package that STAYS installed and is still described by rootfs/manifest.lock — the lock's set is
# unchanged, so genmanifest.sh needs to know nothing about this file (it reads only `pkg` rows,
# from seal.list). If an entire package turns out to be unwanted, remove it from PKGS in
# rootfs/customize-base.sh and relock; do not shadow it with a path row here.
#
# Two row kinds:
#
#   path <p>      a root-relative path removed wholesale (rm -rf). Directories only, by
#                 convention — for a single file, prefer a glob so the row states the shape of
#                 what it removes.
#
#   glob <p>      a root-relative pattern, expanded under the staged root with `extglob` and
#                 `globstar` on. Every match is removed. Expanding to NOTHING is a hard error:
#                 a pattern that matches nothing is a row that has silently stopped doing its
#                 job (the package was dropped, or upstream moved the files), and a trim list
#                 full of dead rows is how this file would rot without anyone noticing.
#
# Both kinds reject an absolute path or one containing `..`, exactly as the sealer does.
#
# RELEASE ONLY, like the seal. A NOVADECK_DEV tree keeps pacman, and deleting files out from
# under a live package database would make on-device `pacman -Qkk` and every reinstall lie about
# the tree. rootfs/assemble-rootfs.sh calls this only on the release path.
#
# NOT trimmed, deliberately:
#   usr/share/licenses — license texts are a shipping obligation, not documentation.
#   usr/lib/locale     — the COMPILED locale (locale-archive) that LANG=en_US.UTF-8 resolves
#                        against at runtime. Only the gettext catalogues below go.
#   usr/lib/gconv      — iconv modules. Small (20M) and reachable from any charset conversion;
#                        not worth the risk of a game or the client asking for one that is gone.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build-time artefacts. A read-only root with no compiler and (after the seal) no package
# manager cannot use a header, a static library, or a build-system description file.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
path usr/include
path usr/lib/cmake
path usr/lib/pkgconfig
path usr/share/pkgconfig
path usr/share/aclocal
# GObject-introspection XML. The RUNTIME format is the .typelib under usr/lib/girepository-*,
# which is what PyGObject loads for novadeck-powerd and novadeck-steamos-manager; the .gir here
# is its source, consumed by g-ir-compiler and by bindings generators at build time.
path usr/share/gir-1.0
# Static libraries — 100M, of which Qt6's libQt6QmlDom.a + libQt6QmlLS.a (QML tooling for an IDE)
# are 41M. Nothing links a .a at runtime, by definition.
glob usr/lib/*.a

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documentation. None of it is reachable from the Deck shell: there is no terminal in the
# release UI, no man(1), and no browser pointed at /usr/share/doc.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
path usr/share/doc
path usr/share/man
path usr/share/info
path usr/share/gtk-doc

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Locale data.
#
# usr/share/i18n holds locale SOURCE definitions, the input to locale-gen. customize-base.sh
# already ran locale-gen at build time (it writes /etc/locale.conf with LANG=en_US.UTF-8 and
# generates the archive), and a read-only root can never run it again — so the sources are inert.
#
# usr/share/locale holds gettext .mo message catalogues: translations of SYSTEM strings. The
# Steam client ships and translates its own UI independently of these, and the release shell is
# Steam. 150M for translated messages of programs the user never sees output from.
#
# The keep list is C, en and en_US — everything /etc/locale.conf can select. Note the extglob:
# `!(...)` is a NEGATED match, so this row means "every locale directory except these".
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
path usr/share/i18n
glob usr/share/locale/!(C|en|en_US)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language runtimes from gcc-libs that nothing on this image links.
#
# gcc-libs is a hard dependency of ~90 packages (glibc, systemd, mesa, qt6, …) so the package
# itself can never be removed — but it ships the runtime for every language GCC can compile,
# and this image runs C, C++ and Rust. Checked against the built tree: outside the libraries
# themselves there is not one NEEDED reference to any of these.
#
# libgcc_s, libstdc++ and libatomic are NOT here: those are the ones that are linked, everywhere.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
glob usr/lib/libgo.so.*
# D: libgphobos is the standard library, libgdruntime the runtime under it. They go together.
glob usr/lib/libgphobos.so.*
glob usr/lib/libgdruntime.so.*
glob usr/lib/libgfortran.so.*
# The sanitizer runtimes (-fsanitize=address/thread/leak/undefined). Development tooling: a
# sanitized binary links them explicitly, and we ship none. `@(...)` is extglob for exactly one
# of the alternatives.
glob usr/lib/lib@(a|t|l|ub)san.so.*
#
# libgomp is deliberately NOT here despite looking like the same kind of thing: it is OpenMP, and
# it IS linked — by libb2, libsoxr, libvidstab and the fftw *_omp variants that ffmpeg pulls in.
# libgcc_s / libstdc++ / libatomic are linked essentially everywhere. libitm and libobjc are
# unreferenced too, but together they are 1.4M and not worth a row.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# podman's remote client.
#
# podman ships two ~40M Go binaries: `podman`, which does the work, and `podman-remote`, a
# client that does NOTHING locally — it only speaks the REST API of a podman service over a
# socket, and reports "unable to connect" without one. We ship podman for exactly one caller,
# Valve's Lepton compat tool (issue #58), which invokes the local `podman` binary directly and
# never starts podman.service or podman.socket (nothing enables either: the stock
# 99-default.preset is "disable *" and the package ships no .wants symlink).
#
# 34M — a quarter of everything the container runtime added — for a binary with no local
# function on this image. Same class as the gcc-libs rows above: the package stays installed
# and stays in rootfs/manifest.lock, only an unreferenced artefact of it goes.
#
# Not removed alongside it: usr/lib/podman/quadlet (2.5M), which the podman-system-generator
# and podman-user-generator DO exec on every boot and every user session.
glob usr/bin/podman-remote
